WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.802
MCP systems can move sensitive data through prompts, resources, files, logs, retrieval results, tool responses, and downstream APIs.

2
00:00:08.802 --> 00:00:12.552
That makes data protection a first-class design concern.

3
00:00:12.552 --> 00:00:22.348
A model context may include customer records, internal documents, code, tickets, telemetry, credentials, regulated information, or personal data.

4
00:00:22.348 --> 00:00:31.686
The fact that the data is being used to help the user does not remove the need for access control, minimization, retention limits, and monitoring.

5
00:00:31.686 --> 00:00:37.962
Resource access should be governed by application policy and identity, not by model confidence.

6
00:00:37.962 --> 00:00:45.003
If a user is not allowed to read a document, the model should not receive that document because it seems useful.

7
00:00:45.003 --> 00:00:55.489
Per-user access filtering, tenant isolation, group membership checks, and resource-level permissions should happen before content enters the model context.

8
00:00:55.489 --> 00:01:05.821
Cross-user or cross-tenant leakage is a severe failure because the model may summarize, combine, or expose information that never should have been available.

9
00:01:05.821 --> 00:01:09.265
Resource provenance helps teams reason about trust.

10
00:01:09.265 --> 00:01:19.062
The system should know where content came from, when it was retrieved, which identity accessed it, and whether it is authoritative for the current task.

11
00:01:19.062 --> 00:01:24.113
Stale or untrusted resources should not silently drive high-impact actions.

12
00:01:24.113 --> 00:01:28.322
Redaction can reduce exposure when full content is unnecessary.

13
00:01:28.322 --> 00:01:37.277
Data minimization asks whether the model needs the whole file, a small excerpt, metadata, or a structured result from a server-side query.

14
00:01:37.277 --> 00:01:39.956
Secrets handling deserves special care.

15
00:01:39.956 --> 00:01:48.911
Tokens, credentials, private keys, session cookies, and connection strings should not be placed in prompts, tool descriptions, or logs.

16
00:01:48.911 --> 00:01:53.732
Local file access should be constrained to intended paths and operations.

17
00:01:53.732 --> 00:01:58.631
Cloud resource access should use least privilege and service-side policy.

18
00:01:58.631 --> 00:02:05.213
Encryption, retention controls, and logging hygiene matter, but they do not replace authorization.

19
00:02:05.213 --> 00:02:11.336
MCP data protection works best when sensitive content is filtered before it reaches the model.
